1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,629
(Q31) For my presentation today, I’m going to talk about the Céide Fields in the northwest of Ireland, one of the largest Neolithic sites in the world.

2
00:00:11,629 --> 00:00:19,117
I recently visited this site and observed the work that is currently being done by a team of archaeologists there.

3
00:00:19,117 --> 00:00:26,094
The site was first discovered in the 1930s by a local teacher, Patrick Caulfield.

4
00:00:26,094 --> 00:00:34,818
He noticed that when local people were digging in the bog, they were constantly hitting against what seemed to be rows of stones.

5
00:00:34,818 --> 00:00:44,687
[Q31答案句] He realised that these must be walls and that they must be thousands of years old for them to predate the bog which subsequently grew over them.

6
00:00:44,687 --> 00:00:51,944
(Q32) He wrote to the National Museum in Dublin to ask them to investigate, but no one took him seriously.

7
00:00:51,944 --> 00:01:02,309
[Q32答案句] It wasn’t until 40 years later, when Patrick Caulfield’s son Seamus, who had become an archaeologist by then, began to explore further.

8
00:01:02,309 --> 00:01:15,918
(Q33) [Q33答案句] He inserted iron probes into the bog to map the formation of the stones, a traditional method which local people had always used for finding fuel buried in the bog for thousands of years.

9
00:01:15,918 --> 00:01:26,130
(Q34) Carbon dating later proved that the site was over 5,000 years old and was the largest Neolithic site in Ireland.

10
00:01:26,130 --> 00:01:37,573
Thanks to the bog which covers the area, the remains of the settlement at Céide Fields, which is over 5,000 years old, are extremely well-preserved.

11
00:01:37,573 --> 00:01:51,382
A bog is 90 percent water; its soil is so saturated that when the grasses and heathers that grow on its surface die, they don’t fully decay but accumulate in layers.

12
00:01:51,382 --> 00:01:59,376
[Q34答案句] Objects remain so well preserved in these conditions because of the acidity of the peat and the deficiency of oxygen.

13
00:01:59,376 --> 00:02:10,994
(Q35) At least 175 days of rain a year are required for this to happen; this part of Ireland gets an average of 225 days.

14
00:02:10,994 --> 00:02:17,588
The Neolithic farmers at Céide would have enjoyed several centuries of relative peace and stability.&nbsp;

15
00:02:17,588 --> 00:02:26,954
Neolithic farmers generally lived in larger communities than their predecessors, with a number of houses built around a community building.

16
00:02:26,954 --> 00:02:33,468
As they lived in permanent settlements, Neolithic farmers were able to build bigger houses.

17
00:02:33,468 --> 00:02:42,259
[Q35答案句] These weren’t round as people often assume, but rectangular with a small hole in the roof that allowed smoke to escape.

18
00:02:42,259 --> 00:02:50,682
(Q36) This is one of many innovations and indicates that the Neolithic farmers were the first people to cook indoors.

19
00:02:50,682 --> 00:02:56,035
Another new technology that Neolithic settlers brought to Ireland was pottery.

20
00:02:56,035 --> 00:03:01,666
Fragments of Neolithic pots have been found in Céide and elsewhere in Ireland.

21
00:03:01,666 --> 00:03:13,639
[Q36答案句] The pots were used for many things; as well as for storing food, pots were filled with a small amount of fat and when this was set alight, they served as lamps.

22
00:03:13,639 --> 00:03:20,385
(Q37) It’s thought that the Céide Fields were mainly used as paddocks for animals to graze in.

23
00:03:20,385 --> 00:03:28,260
[Q37答案句] Evidence from the Céide Fields suggests that each plot of land was of a suitable size to sustain an extended family.

24
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:38,135
(Q38) They may have used a system of rotational grazing in order to prevent over-grazing and to allow for plant recovery and regrowth.

25
00:03:38,135 --> 00:03:45,720
[Q38答案句] This must have been a year-round activity as no structures have been found which would have been used to shelter animals in the winter.

26
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:53,519
(Q39) However, archaeologists believe that this way of life at Céide ceased abruptly.

27
00:03:53,519 --> 00:03:54,746
Why was this?

28
00:03:54,746 --> 00:03:59,600
Well, several factors may have contributed to the changing circumstances.

29
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:04,858
[Q39答案句] The soil would have become less productive and led to the abandonment of farming.

30
00:04:04,858 --> 00:04:12,678
(Q40) The crop rotationsystem was partly responsible for this as it would have been very intensive and was not sustainable.

31
00:04:12,678 --> 00:04:15,849
But there were also climatic pressures too.

32
00:04:15,849 --> 00:04:26,122
[Q40答案句] The farmers at Céide would have enjoyed a relatively dry period, but this began to change and the conditions became wetter as there was a lot more rain.&nbsp;

33
00:04:26,122 --> 00:04:33,002
It was these conditions that encouraged the bog to form over the area which survives today.

34
00:04:33,002 --> 00:04:35,502
So now I'd like to show you some...

